WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
A red blight moves into a forest where the trees look alike.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.120
What decides whether this population continues or disappears?

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.520
Watch the trees, their blue resistance bars, and the yellow seeds that mark a next

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.520
generation.

5
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:22.603
After a change, some members of a population die and some live.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.483
Living members may reproduce.

7
00:00:26.823 --> 00:00:30.443
Some migrate, meaning they move elsewhere, where they must still survive.

8
00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:35.763
A species is extinct only when no living members remain anywhere.

9
00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.323
Losing a species from one forest is not the same thing.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.013
Now look inside each tree.

11
00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:48.973
The blue bar represents inherited resistance, the ability to hold off the blight.

12
00:00:50.313 --> 00:00:53.953
Different bar heights show genetic variation in this useful trait.

13
00:00:55.073 --> 00:00:58.173
The tree cannot raise its bar because danger arrives.

14
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.953
It begins with traits inherited from its parents.

15
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:05.057
Red cankers spread through the bark.

16
00:01:06.597 --> 00:01:09.197
Trees with shorter blue bars are more likely to fade.

17
00:01:10.477 --> 00:01:12.637
A taller bar does not promise survival.

18
00:01:13.837 --> 00:01:15.337
It only changes the chance.

19
00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:21.717
Survivors that make yellow seeds can pass inherited traits into the next generation.

20
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.280
Compare these forests.

21
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.900
Every tree in the narrow forest has a short resistance bar.

22
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.560
The varied forest includes short, middle, and taller bars.

23
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.100
Which forest is more likely to leave seed-making survivors after the blight?

24
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:50.073
The varied forest has a better chance because some helpful differences were already

25
00:01:50.073 --> 00:01:53.593
present. Yet it could still lose every tree.

26
00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:58.333
Variation in leaf shape or height would not solve this problem.

27
00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:01.473
The difference must help with this change.

28
00:02:02.793 --> 00:02:07.813
Helpful differences can appear later or arrive from elsewhere, but a fast change may

29
00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:08.893
leave too little time.

30
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:12.093
Green trees remain after the blight.

31
00:02:13.293 --> 00:02:17.293
If they never make seeds, their population still has no next generation.

32
00:02:18.813 --> 00:02:20.773
Now yellow seeds fall and seedlings rise.

33
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:28.313
Here is the rule. When environments change, populations survive only if some members

34
00:02:28.313 --> 00:02:29.673
survive and reproduce.

35
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.737
A bird can leave a changed place.

36
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:37.917
A rooted chestnut cannot, though seeds may spread its kind over time.

37
00:02:39.397 --> 00:02:44.017
Habitat loss, pollution, climate change, and invasive species create different

38
00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:49.457
challenges. The same test remains: can any members survive and reproduce?

39
00:02:50.617 --> 00:02:52.377
Follow the forest once more.

40
00:02:53.757 --> 00:02:54.757
Red blight arrives.

41
00:02:55.977 --> 00:02:57.197
Blue resistance differs.

42
00:02:58.617 --> 00:03:02.037
Some trees remain, and yellow seeds start another generation.

43
00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:07.677
A population continues only when living members make a next generation.

44
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:11.677
This rule names a requirement, not a guarantee.

45
00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:15.337
A model forest is not a real forest.

46
00:03:16.777 --> 00:03:18.857
Next, test the rule in a model forest.

47
00:03:20.117 --> 00:03:22.777
Then read the real American chestnut data below.
